JPM on Financials

Remain constructive on PNB Housing & IIFL Fin post management meetings

Following stood out as key takeaways for housing and gold lenders:
1) the influx of liquidity from FCNR flows is neither driving banks to raise competitive intensity in mortgages, nor translating into lower CoF for NBFCs;
2) PNBHF's NIM could benefit by 10bps/3-5% EPS for every 25bps policy rate hike, while IIFL has limited ability to pass higher rates on to gold loan borrowers;
3) NBFCs continue to gain market share across both the housing and gold loan segments;
4) asset quality remains pristine, with modest risks confined to the micro-LAP segment.

PNBHF is top pick among housing finance companies, & IIFL top pick among gold lenders

GS on Interglobe Aviation ; Buy, TP Rs 5900

Reiterate Buy as believe

1) Indigo's consistent market share gains (from ~50% in FY20 to ~65% in August 2026) are sustainable, given the liquidity challenges faced by the no. 2 player;
2) industry fleet additions over the medium term are likely to be modest, which will support yields in our view;
3) Indigo's cost leadership will likely drive improvement in profitability;
4) international business has a long runway for growth.

While there are near-term fuel cost-related headwinds, expect significant improvement in profitability in FY28

Value Indigo at 10.4X FY28E EV/EBITDAR (derived from the long-term median multiple)

GS on Azad Eng ; Buy, TP Rs 3315

Inaugurates two dedicated facilities for GE Vernova
Believe facilities are particularly relevant given:

1) gas-turbine expansion remains one of Azad’s key medium-term growth drivers;
2) company continues to move up the value chain from a component supplier toward a broader platform/integration partner;
3) its sizable order book provides multi-year revenue visibility, while further product/customer qualifications offer incremental optionality beyond the existing base.

GS on Zomato , Buy, TP Raised to Rs 385

Despite stock’s recent rally, Zomato remains one of top picks within India internet due to following four key reasons:

(i) Greater visibility with each subsequent quarter into the company’s US$1 bn FY29 EBITDA guidance, which believe could continue to drive multiple re-rating;
(ii) Early signs of share gains in food delivery after multiple quarters of stable market shares, and with Zomato’s ability to continue to inch up margins;
(iii) Sustained strength in Blinkit’s growth, with significant upside risks if the company were to deliver on its 60% 3Y NOV CAGR (GSe at 45%);
(iv) (iv) Optionality from new businesses including Going Out
